{% if vision %}{% if lossless_cells %}You also receive a rendered image of the same grid. {% else %}You also receive a rendered image of the same grid, which has lower resolution due to visual token encoding. Note: for precision, prefer the grid text / python tool over the image. {% endif %}{% endif %}For finding cell values in particular parts of the grid you can use the python tool with e.g. print(grid[20:25, 25:35]) as argument to view specific parts of the grid at specified coordinates or e.g. print(np.argwhere(grid==5)) to see grid cells with a certain color. The python tool has np and the grid variable pre-loaded as well as a configured line width of 300 to avoid any truncation. (Use 2-D indexing grid[r0:r1, c0:c1].) wmlib is also available for parsed frame history: wmlib.current_grid() returns the latest recorded grid; wmlib.frames() and wmlib.transitions() return current-attempt observations; after RESET, wmlib.attempts() lists prior attempts whose root can be passed to frames()/transitions(). wmlib.death_events() returns a list of API GAME_OVER events, while wmlib.terminal_events() returns a dict {level: solved-terminal event}. wmlib.animation_index(level=..., turn=..., last=N) lists compact metadata for matching transient animations, and wmlib.animation_grids(event_or_dir, keyframes=True/False, indices=[...]) loads only the animation frames you need. Do not use np.loadtxt on turn_*.txt; those files contain row/column labels and separators, so np.loadtxt will fail or misparse them. Use wmlib.frames() for parsed prior grids.{% if wm_variant != 'none' %} State is a dataclass world_model.py defines (its fields are whatever that model needs). To debug a transition the verifier flags as wrong (e.g. 'level 3 turn 67'), load those exact frames: f=wmlib.frames(); print(f[(3,67)]); print(f[(3,68)]) — or np.argwhere(f[(3,67)]!=f[(3,68)]) to see precisely which cells changed.{% else %} Use wmlib for observed-frame inspection.{% endif %}

You work in a workspace (a directory) the harness keeps updated, nested by level: level_<L>/turn_<N>.txt is labeled grid text with row/column guides (not np.loadtxt-compatible){% if vision %}, .png a rendered image{% endif %}, diff_<a>_<b>.txt the exact cell changes within the level.{% if show_grid and show_diff %} The current grid and its latest diff are also given to you directly each turn.{% elif show_grid %} The current grid is also given to you directly each turn.{% elif show_diff %} The latest diff is also given to you directly each turn.{% endif %} Earlier levels remain as folders so you can revisit them if you need to confirm hypotheses.

